# Design Plan — Next-Generation Pure Electric Sedan Battery (t1_r1)

## 1. Objective decomposition (parsed verbatim, headless)
- ED ≥ 392.61 Wh/kg — stage2, calc-energy contract (stack mass: electrodes+CC+separator; electrolyte/casing excluded — noted honestly in report).
- 4C fast charge, no Li plating — stage3: plated=false on 4C_charge_45C protocol (45°C ambient, 900 s), anode_potential_v ≥ 0 V.
- Max temperature ≤ 60°C — stage3: T_max_K ≤ 333.15 (60+273.15, mechanical conversion) on 4C protocol with coupled lumped thermal.
- Overcharge to 4.7 V, no thermal runaway — stage3: triggered=false via run-pyamm overcharge (4.2 V cut-off +0.5 V = 4.7 V) → run-tr ODE coupling (cell mass from calc-energy).

Trade-off map:
- ED vs 4C: thicker/compacted electrodes ↑ED but ↑ polarization → plating & heat. Thin CC/separator ↑ED without rate penalty (primary ED lever).
- Plating vs Tmax: faster transport (high σ_e, small particles) helps both; higher h helps Tmax only.
- Overcharge TR: cell thermal mass (mcp), overcharge heat (impedance), heat rejection (hA) are the levers; cathode oxidative stability at 4.7 V is the material-level risk.

## 2. Candidate strategy
- R0 baseline: Chen2020 defaults, all four protocols → anchor.
- Ceiling probe: Chen2020 best-architecture (thin CC 6µm/4µm, separator 8-10µm, thicker electrodes if 4C allows, lower porosity, small anode particles, high-σ electrolyte) — check whether 392.61 & 4C coexist.
- If ceiling < target or 4C plating persists → escalate to Stage 2: system candidate OKane2022 (SiOx anode, plating/cracking params) and/or electrolyte formulation candidates (σ/t⁺/D overrides).
- Safety fine-tuning: h (cooling design) for Tmax; N/P + particle size for plating; hA/mass for TR.

## 3. Budget allocation
R0 1 round; architecture 3-5 rounds; Stage-2 escalation 2-3 rounds if needed; safety fine-tune 2; deliverables fixed cost.

## 4. Risk & fallback
(as above)

## 5. References (domain basis — real sources)
- Si/SiOx anodes raise capacity & lithiation potential (plating margin): Obrovac & Chevrier, "Alloy Negative Electrodes for Li-Ion Batteries", Chem. Rev. 2014, 114, 11444–11502.
- Electrolyte transport & interphases: Xu, "Electrolytes and Interphases in Li-Ion Batteries and Beyond", Chem. Rev. 2014, 114, 11503–11618.
- Overcharge/TR mechanisms: Feng, Ouyang et al., "Thermal runaway mechanism of lithium ion battery for electric vehicles: A review", Energy Storage Mater. 2018, 10, 246–267.
- Fast-charge plating criterion (anode potential < 0 V vs Li/Li+): domain experience (electrochemistry standard).
- Cooling h ranges (air ~5-10, liquid plate ~50-200 W/m²K): domain experience.

